  "notes": "Mobile, AL — bratty and precise, and built on sample flips, which is the parameter that matters: the research says the key is *whatever the flipped sample supplies*, frequently a major or modal pop/R&B loop giving genuine 4-bar progressions. So this entry carries real major families, a `1_per_bar` harmonic rhythm at the top weight, and `phraseBars` weighted to 4 rather than trap's 2 — the loop is the sample's loop, not a two-bar ostinato. The bell counterline the research names is why `countermelody.densityRatio` is 0.55, the highest in this batch bar Swae Lee: it is a written second part, not an echo. ⚠ Authored on the 95–110 straight-feel bounce with the clap on 2 and 4; the 130–145 half-time cuts the research also names are not modelled. ⚠ `confidence` medium — the sample-flip construction is legible from the catalog but workflow sourcing is thin. The INFERRED parameters are the drum internals: `kick.syncopation` 0.55, `snare.layerClapOffsetMs` [1,3] and `hihat.fillDensity` 0.6 come from \"bouncy kick\", \"snappy layered claps\" and \"straight 16th hats with triplet inserts\" — prose, not figures. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
